According to the NHS, iron is important in making red blood cells, which carry oxygen around the body (http://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). Good sources of iron include liver, red meat, beans, nuts, dried fruit, fortified breakfast cereals and soy bean flour (http://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). Additional sources listed by the NHS are dark‑green leafy vegetables, fortified cereals and bread, meat, dried fruit such as apricots, prunes and raisins, and pulses (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/Anaemia-iron-deficiency-). The recommended daily intake for UK adults is 8.7 mg for men aged 19 and over and for women aged 50 and over, and 14.8 mg for women aged 19 to 49 (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).

