Iron is important in making red blood cells, which carry oxygen around the body (NHS). It is found in red meat, fortified breakfast cereals, beans and pulses such as lentils, kidney beans, baked beans and chickpeas, green vegetables such as spinach and broccoli, nuts and seeds such as almonds, Brazil nuts, hazelnuts, sesame seeds and sunflower seeds, dried apricots, and tofu (NHS Kingston and Richmond). Animal‑based sources such as liver, kidney, red meat and sardines contain the highest iron (NHS Kingston and Richmond). The UK reference intake for adult men is 8.7 mg per day, for women aged 19‑49 years is 14.8 mg per day, and for women aged 50 years and over is 8.7 mg per day (NHS). Side effects of doses above 20 mg may include constipation, nausea, vomiting and stomach pain (NHS).

