Iron is important for the formation of red blood cells and for transporting oxygen in the body, and it also occurs in enzymes that help produce muscle proteins and support the immune system (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).  
Rich dietary sources of iron include liver, kidney, red meat, sardines, fortified breakfast cereals, beans, pulses, green vegetables, nuts, seeds, dried fruit and tofu (Kingston and Richmond NHS, https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet-sheet).  
The recommended daily intake for UK adults is 8.7 mg for men aged 19 and over and for women aged 50 and over, and 14.8 mg for women aged 19 to 49 (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).  
The upper safety limit for iron intake is 17 mg per day (NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).  
Prescribing of oral iron tablets is generally not recommended in primary care (BSW Formulary, https://bswformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.01.01&SubSectionID=A100).

